## Tensor device execution (Phase 0: abstraction + fallback)
##
## Part of the Alya Language package ecosystem (https://github.com/alya-lang).
##
## Device placement API for tensor compute offload. The Alya side is fully
## wired: tensors carry a `device_id`, `to()` stages explicit transfers,
## and `device_has_accelerator()` queries the native registry in
## `c/device.c`. No platform kernel is registered yet (count is 0 on every
## OS), so compute always runs the CPU/SIMD kernels — loudly documented,
## never a silent fake GPU. Platform backends (Metal / CUDA / OpenCL)
## plug into the registry without changing user code.

extern "C"
    function alya_tensor_device_count() -> i32
end

## Reports whether any accelerator device is usable on this machine.
##
## Queries the native registry (`c/device.c`). Returns `false` everywhere
## until a platform backend registers; code can branch on it to pick
## transfer strategies.
##
## ### Returns
## `true` when at least one accelerator device is available.
pub function device_has_accelerator() -> bool
    if alya_tensor_device_count() > 0
        return true
    end
    return false
end

## Global ordering barrier for device streams.
##
## Phase 0 execution is synchronous, so this is a documented no-op.
## Forward-compatible code calls it after enqueueing device work; once
## async backends land, it becomes the fence without API changes.
pub function synchronize()
end
